  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nEarly this morning storms continue to occur to our south across\nIowa, but storm motions and the overall forcing driving these\nstorms will keep them well to our south. Today will see the\nstart of the main push of the WAA in the afternoon/evening. This\nmeans right as the diurnal temperatures would normally start to\nfall this WAA will counteract this somewhat. This strong flow is\ntied to a low moving near the international border region around\nthe AB/SK/MT/ND area. This is what gives us the flow driving\nthis WAA. We will see the peak of this WAA on Saturday with 850\nmb temperatures of 20 to 25 degrees C. This very warm air aloft\nwill likely make Saturday start out with a temperature inversion\nwhich will limit wind potential early. This will not last\nthough as the atmosphere warms during the day and mixing becomes\nfavored. By afternoon wind gusts up around 40 mph will be\npossible, most likely along the Buffalo Ridge. With respect to\nfire weather, winds are a concern and so are the near record \nhigh temperatures. What will decide how much of a fire risk we \nget will be RHs, as current dew points in the NBM make this look\nborderline. However NBM often does not do well in these kind of\ndays and there is a decent chance that the HRRR is closer to \nreality and this would suggest western Minnesota falling into \nthe mid to upper 20s for dew points.\n\nThe WAA continues into Sunday with another much above normal day\nexpected. It will be late Sunday into Monday when we see a cold\nfront, associated with the low near the international border, \nmoves across the Upper Midwest and bring this abnormal warmth to\nan end. The actual frontal passage won't take a day and a half,\nthere just remains some spread in the timing of this passage in\nguidance. Currently only low PoPs with this frontal passage as \nthere is little upper level support. Even in the guidance that \nshows rain with this front it is only a few hundredths due this\nweaker forcing setup and questionable low level moisture. After\na weekend with highs in the 80s the post-frontal air mass will \nsupport near normal highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Another \nfrontal passage Wednesday night into Thursday will give us our \nbest chance for rain over the next week and another drop in \ntemperatures. Unlike the early week frontal passage this one has\nample upper level support with a rather pronounced trough \nmoving across CONUS. This shows much stronger support in global \nensembles, compared to Monday, with a strong signal. There are\neven members with a more soaking rain of a half inch or more. \nCurrently the stronger signal remains to our northwest, but some\nadditional shifts in track at this range is possible. Behind \nthis front, high will likely only be in the lower to mid 50s on \nFriday.",
